1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:03,500
Nearly three thousand years ago,

2
00:00:03,540 --> 00:00:08,910
the story goes, a queen in exile named
Dido landed on the coast of North Africa.

3
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:12,120
She asked the local king
for land to build a city.

4
00:00:12,270 --> 00:00:15,210
He offered her a joke:
as much ground as she could

5
00:00:15,260 --> 00:00:18,010
surround with the hide of a single ox.

6
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:22,137
So she cut the hide into strips,
thin as thread,

7
00:00:22,187 --> 00:00:25,110
and tied them into one enormous rope.

8
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:29,100
Then she walked to the shore, because
the sea would guard one side for free.

9
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:33,340
Only one question was left.
What shape should the rope make?

10
00:00:33,970 --> 00:00:38,620
That is a very human question.
Not, what is the best thing in the world?

11
00:00:38,670 --> 00:00:41,670
But, what is the best we
can do with what we have?

12
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:44,200
A fixed length of rope.
A fixed budget.

13
00:00:44,250 --> 00:00:48,290
A bridge that must stand,
using as little steel as possible.

14
00:00:48,890 --> 00:00:51,750
Around sixteen thirty-six,
Pierre de Fermat found

15
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:55,880
a rule for the easier kind of problem,
the kind where nothing holds you back.

16
00:00:55,930 --> 00:00:58,940
At the very top of a hill,
the ground goes flat.

17
00:00:58,990 --> 00:01:02,290
Find the flat places,
and you have found your candidates.

18
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:04,550
But limits break that rule.

19
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:07,430
Imagine hiking a trail
that loops around a mountain.

20
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:11,810
The summit is right there, up the slope,
but you have to stay on the path.

21
00:01:11,860 --> 00:01:14,250
So where on this path are you highest?

22
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:17,790
Stand at that spot,
and the ground is not flat at all.

23
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:21,840
The mountain still rises beside you.
You just can't go that way.

24
00:01:22,530 --> 00:01:25,320
The person who cracked
this was a young man from Turin.

25
00:01:25,370 --> 00:01:29,123
In seventeen fifty-five,
at just nineteen, Joseph-Louis

26
00:01:29,173 --> 00:01:33,017
Lagrange wrote to Leonhard Euler,
the leading mathematician of the age,

27
00:01:33,067 --> 00:01:36,060
with a new method for problems
very much like Dido's.

28
00:01:36,110 --> 00:01:39,497
Thirty-three years later,
in his masterpiece on mechanics,

29
00:01:39,547 --> 00:01:43,040
he worked out how machines balance
when their parts hold each other back.

30
00:01:43,090 --> 00:01:47,390
And to each of those restrictions,
he gave one extra unknown number.

31
00:01:47,860 --> 00:01:50,367
We call those numbers
Lagrange multipliers,

32
00:01:50,393 --> 00:01:52,390
and today they are everywhere.

33
00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:56,580
In the design of wings and bridges.
In how economists value a budget.

34
00:01:56,630 --> 00:02:00,940
In how a network shares out its power,
how machines learn to draw boundaries,

35
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:03,110
and even in the laws of heat.

36
00:02:03,610 --> 00:02:06,630
Lagrange was so confident
in his algebra that the preface

37
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:11,200
of that book makes a striking promise:
no figures will be found in this book.

38
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:12,730
Not one picture.

39
00:02:12,810 --> 00:02:14,790
Which is a pity, because underneath

40
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:18,490
his algebra is one of the most
beautiful pictures in mathematics.

41
00:02:18,980 --> 00:02:20,160
So let's draw it.

42
00:02:20,210 --> 00:02:24,250
We'll see what happens at the highest
point of that trail, why one arrow

43
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:28,277
is forced to line up with another,
how that picture becomes a method,

44
00:02:28,327 --> 00:02:30,700
and how the method solves real problems.

45
00:02:30,750 --> 00:02:31,957
And by the end,

46
00:02:32,007 --> 00:02:36,120
that mysterious extra number will turn
into something surprisingly concrete.

47
00:02:36,170 --> 00:02:39,330
On Dido's beach,
you can measure it with a stick.

48
00:02:41,030 --> 00:02:43,440
Let's turn the mountain into mathematics.

49
00:02:43,490 --> 00:02:47,110
Give every point on the map a height,
f of x and y.

50
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:50,600
This is the quantity we want
to make as large as possible.

51
00:02:50,650 --> 00:02:52,580
We call it the objective.

52
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:56,030
With no limits,
the search is easy to describe.

53
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:59,890
Stand anywhere and ask which
way is steepest uphill.

54
00:03:00,010 --> 00:03:04,270
That direction, with a length that says
how steep, is the gradient of f.

55
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:06,130
Follow it and you climb.

56
00:03:06,180 --> 00:03:10,890
You stop only when the arrow shrinks
to nothing, when the gradient is zero.

57
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:13,230
That is Fermat's flat ground.

58
00:03:13,890 --> 00:03:15,440
Now add the trail.

59
00:03:15,490 --> 00:03:19,390
A trail is a rule about where you
are allowed to be, and we can write

60
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:25,310
that rule as an equation: some function
g of x and y must equal a constant, c.

61
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:28,310
Points that satisfy
the rule are on the path.

62
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:30,160
Everything else is forbidden.

63
00:03:30,760 --> 00:03:32,130
Look at what that does.

64
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:36,980
You had two directions of freedom,
and now you have one: forward or back.

65
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:40,500
Sometimes you can write the path
as a formula and reduce everything

66
00:03:40,540 --> 00:03:42,060
to a single variable.

67
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:43,887
But real constraints are tangled,

68
00:03:43,913 --> 00:03:47,420
and that substitution quickly
becomes ugly or impossible.

69
00:03:47,460 --> 00:03:50,830
We want a way to find the answer
without unrolling the trail.

70
00:03:52,350 --> 00:03:56,560
So let's look at the mountain the way
a hiker does: from above, on a map.

71
00:03:56,610 --> 00:04:02,130
Slice it at equal heights, and each slice
leaves a contour line, a level curve of f.

72
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:05,760
Walk along one contour
and your height never changes.

73
00:04:06,070 --> 00:04:08,410
That tells us something
about the gradient.

74
00:04:08,490 --> 00:04:10,847
Moving along a contour gains nothing,

75
00:04:10,873 --> 00:04:13,640
so the uphill arrow has
no part pointing along it.

76
00:04:13,690 --> 00:04:17,820
The gradient is always perpendicular
to the contour beneath your feet.

77
00:04:18,420 --> 00:04:21,160
Now walk the trail and watch the contours.

78
00:04:21,210 --> 00:04:23,350
Here, the trail cuts across them,

79
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:27,050
one after another,
and every crossing means you are climbing.

80
00:04:27,190 --> 00:04:29,440
Keep going.
The crossings spread out.

81
00:04:29,490 --> 00:04:31,230
The climb is getting gentler.

82
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:35,200
And then, at one special point,
the trail stops crossing.

83
00:04:35,250 --> 00:04:39,910
It touches a contour, runs alongside
it for an instant, and falls away.

84
00:04:40,530 --> 00:04:43,610
That is the top,
and it has to look like this.

85
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:45,860
If the trail crossed the contour there,

86
00:04:45,900 --> 00:04:49,150
one direction would carry you higher,
and you wouldn't be at the top.

87
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:51,190
Here is another way to see it.

88
00:04:51,240 --> 00:04:53,250
Start with a high contour,

89
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:56,930
a small loop near the summit
that doesn't reach the trail at all.

90
00:04:56,980 --> 00:04:58,750
Now lower the height.

91
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:03,880
The loop swells outward until,
at one moment, it first touches the path.

92
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:06,960
That first touch is the highest
point you can reach.

93
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:12,040
And here is the quiet insight.
The trail is itself a contour.

94
00:05:12,090 --> 00:05:14,510
It is one level curve of g.

95
00:05:14,620 --> 00:05:19,010
So the whole problem is two families
of curves, and the answer is where one

96
00:05:19,060 --> 00:05:21,410
curve from each family just kiss.

97
00:05:22,850 --> 00:05:25,700
Two curves that kiss share a tangent line.

98
00:05:25,750 --> 00:05:29,420
Each curve's gradient
is perpendicular to its own contour.

99
00:05:29,470 --> 00:05:33,707
So at that point, both gradients
are perpendicular to the same line,

100
00:05:33,733 --> 00:05:37,890
and in the plane, that forces them
to point along one common direction.

101
00:05:38,250 --> 00:05:41,180
One arrow must be a multiple of the other.

102
00:05:41,230 --> 00:05:44,880
The gradient of f equals
lambda times the gradient of g.

103
00:05:45,260 --> 00:05:48,120
That Greek letter
is the Lagrange multiplier.

104
00:05:48,170 --> 00:05:50,700
For now, it is just a stretch factor:

105
00:05:50,750 --> 00:05:53,760
how many times longer one
arrow is than the other.

106
00:05:54,530 --> 00:05:56,620
Let's feel why this has to be true.

107
00:05:56,670 --> 00:06:01,100
Walk the trail carrying the uphill arrow,
and split it into two parts:

108
00:06:01,150 --> 00:06:04,030
one along the trail,
and one straight across it.

109
00:06:04,080 --> 00:06:08,480
The part across the trail is useless,
because it points somewhere you can't go.

110
00:06:08,530 --> 00:06:11,730
The part along the trail
tells you to keep walking.

111
00:06:11,780 --> 00:06:15,220
As long as that part exists,
you can still do better.

112
00:06:15,270 --> 00:06:18,970
You arrive at the best point
exactly when it vanishes,

113
00:06:19,020 --> 00:06:22,380
and all that remains of the gradient
points straight across the path.

114
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:25,340
Now the proof, in three lines.

115
00:06:25,390 --> 00:06:29,640
Describe your walk as a path r of t,
with velocity r prime.

116
00:06:29,690 --> 00:06:34,750
You stay on the trail, so g never changes,
and the chain rule says the gradient of g

117
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:36,530
dot r prime is zero.

118
00:06:36,580 --> 00:06:40,670
At the top, your height has stopped
changing, so the gradient of f dot

119
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:42,440
r prime is zero too.

120
00:06:42,770 --> 00:06:46,430
Both gradients are perpendicular
to the same velocity, so as long

121
00:06:46,480 --> 00:06:50,460
as the gradient of g isn't zero,
one is a multiple of the other.

122
00:06:51,080 --> 00:06:52,330
Count what we have.

123
00:06:52,380 --> 00:06:55,290
Two equations from the arrows,
one from the constraint.

124
00:06:55,340 --> 00:07:00,840
Three unknowns: x, y and lambda.
The picture has become algebra.

125
00:07:02,470 --> 00:07:05,440
Lagrange packed all
of this into a single function.

126
00:07:05,490 --> 00:07:08,920
Take the objective and subtract
lambda times the constraint:

127
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:12,580
L equals f minus lambda times g minus c.

128
00:07:13,040 --> 00:07:14,210
Set its derivatives

129
00:07:14,260 --> 00:07:18,130
with respect to x and y to zero,
and you get the arrow equations.

130
00:07:18,180 --> 00:07:20,860
Set its derivative
with respect to lambda to zero,

131
00:07:20,910 --> 00:07:23,050
and out comes the constraint itself.

132
00:07:23,100 --> 00:07:25,050
A restricted problem has become

133
00:07:25,100 --> 00:07:27,800
an unrestricted one,
with one more variable.

134
00:07:27,850 --> 00:07:31,760
Fermat's flat ground has returned,
in a higher dimension.

135
00:07:32,330 --> 00:07:36,110
But be careful.
That flat point is not a peak of L.

136
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:38,270
Take the smallest possible example:

137
00:07:38,320 --> 00:07:42,550
maximize minus x squared,
with x forced to equal one.

138
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:44,650
Plot L over x and lambda,

139
00:07:44,700 --> 00:07:49,090
and the solution sits on a saddle,
rising one way and falling the other.

140
00:07:49,140 --> 00:07:52,860
You can't simply climb to it.
You have to solve for it.

141
00:07:54,590 --> 00:07:56,750
Let's put this to work on Dido's beach.

142
00:07:56,800 --> 00:08:02,280
Take forty meters of rope, a straight
shore, and for now, a rectangular plot.

143
00:08:02,330 --> 00:08:07,270
Two sides of length x leave the shore,
and one side of length y runs along it.

144
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:12,670
We want to maximize the area, x y,
while two x plus y equals forty.

145
00:08:13,260 --> 00:08:18,680
The gradient of the area is y, x.
The gradient of the rope is two, one.

146
00:08:18,680 --> 00:08:22,160
So y equals two lambda,
and x equals lambda.

147
00:08:22,210 --> 00:08:25,670
Put those into the constraint,
and four lambda equals forty.

148
00:08:25,720 --> 00:08:27,100
Lambda is ten.

149
00:08:27,150 --> 00:08:32,430
The plot is ten meters deep, twenty wide,
and encloses two hundred square meters.

150
00:08:32,540 --> 00:08:34,940
Hold on to that ten.
We will need it.

151
00:08:35,630 --> 00:08:38,450
Next,
a case where the picture earns its keep.

152
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:41,370
Find the extremes of x
squared y on the circle

153
00:08:41,420 --> 00:08:43,570
x squared plus y squared equals three.

154
00:08:43,620 --> 00:08:47,560
The arrow equations are two
x y equals two lambda x,

155
00:08:47,610 --> 00:08:50,050
and x squared equals two lambda y.

156
00:08:50,630 --> 00:08:55,230
The first one splits in two.
Either x is zero, or lambda equals y.

157
00:08:55,280 --> 00:08:59,050
If lambda equals y,
then x squared is two y squared,

158
00:08:59,100 --> 00:09:03,430
so y is plus or minus one,
and x is plus or minus root two.

159
00:09:03,480 --> 00:09:08,630
If x is zero, then y is plus or minus
root three, and lambda is zero.

160
00:09:08,970 --> 00:09:13,400
That is six candidates, and six places
where a contour kisses the circle.

161
00:09:13,450 --> 00:09:19,570
Walk around, and f reads two, zero,
two, minus two, zero, minus two.

162
00:09:19,620 --> 00:09:22,830
The maximum is two.
The minimum is minus two.

163
00:09:22,880 --> 00:09:25,860
The two zeros are only
local turning points.

164
00:09:25,910 --> 00:09:28,170
The equations never
tell you which is which.

165
00:09:28,220 --> 00:09:31,240
They hand you every tangency,
and you compare.

166
00:09:32,780 --> 00:09:35,600
Nothing here depends
on living on a flat map.

167
00:09:35,650 --> 00:09:39,150
In three dimensions,
level curves become level surfaces.

168
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:41,630
Suppose we want the point on the plane x

169
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:45,870
plus two y plus two z equals nine
that is closest to the origin.

170
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:48,500
The level surfaces
of distance are spheres.

171
00:09:48,550 --> 00:09:51,480
Inflate a sphere until it
just touches the plane.

172
00:09:51,530 --> 00:09:56,180
At that touch, the two surfaces share
a tangent plane, their gradients align,

173
00:09:56,230 --> 00:09:58,360
and the equation is exactly the same.

174
00:09:58,410 --> 00:10:02,470
The point is one, two, two,
at a distance of three.

175
00:10:03,050 --> 00:10:04,690
Now add a second constraint.

176
00:10:04,740 --> 00:10:09,720
A cylinder and a tilted plane cut out
an ellipse, and we want its highest point.

177
00:10:09,780 --> 00:10:13,967
We are back on a one-dimensional trail,
but now it is floating in space,

178
00:10:13,993 --> 00:10:17,960
and at every point a whole plane
of directions is perpendicular to it.

179
00:10:18,490 --> 00:10:22,310
So the gradient of f no longer has
to line up with a single arrow.

180
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:26,840
It only has to lie in the plane spanned
by the two constraint gradients.

181
00:10:26,890 --> 00:10:28,390
The gradient of f equals

182
00:10:28,440 --> 00:10:32,760
lambda times the gradient of g,
plus mu times the gradient of h.

183
00:10:32,940 --> 00:10:35,460
Every constraint gets its own multiplier.

184
00:10:37,050 --> 00:10:39,350
So what is this number, really?

185
00:10:39,610 --> 00:10:42,310
Go back to the rope.
Lambda was ten.

186
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:45,580
Now add one more meter of rope,
and solve again.

187
00:10:45,630 --> 00:10:49,810
The best area climbs from two
hundred to about two hundred and ten.

188
00:10:50,100 --> 00:10:53,280
Ten more square meters,
for one more meter of rope.

189
00:10:53,900 --> 00:10:55,770
That is no coincidence.

190
00:10:55,820 --> 00:10:59,027
As the constraint loosens,
the best point slides,

191
00:10:59,053 --> 00:11:03,480
and the chain rule shows that the best
value changes at exactly rate lambda.

192
00:11:03,570 --> 00:11:07,580
The multiplier is the exchange rate
between the constraint and the objective.

193
00:11:08,220 --> 00:11:10,430
Economists call it a shadow price.

194
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:13,557
It is what one more unit of budget,
or steel,

195
00:11:13,607 --> 00:11:18,120
or machine time would be worth to you,
even though nobody ever posted that price.

196
00:11:18,670 --> 00:11:23,940
In physics, the same number is a force.
A bead rests at the bottom of a hoop.

197
00:11:24,040 --> 00:11:26,740
Gravity pulls it down
and the hoop pushes back.

198
00:11:26,790 --> 00:11:29,070
Minimize the bead's energy on the hoop,

199
00:11:29,120 --> 00:11:32,800
and lambda times the gradient
of g is exactly that push.

200
00:11:32,850 --> 00:11:36,360
A constraint, physically,
is something pushing back.

201
00:11:36,410 --> 00:11:39,890
That is the world where Lagrange
first found these numbers.

202
00:11:40,420 --> 00:11:42,450
And then the deepest example.

203
00:11:42,620 --> 00:11:46,500
Take a box of gas, with its molecules
sharing a fixed amount of energy.

204
00:11:46,550 --> 00:11:49,970
Of all the ways to share it,
the overwhelmingly likely one

205
00:11:50,020 --> 00:11:51,997
has the highest entropy.

206
00:11:52,047 --> 00:11:53,517
So maximize entropy,

207
00:11:53,567 --> 00:11:57,110
with the total probability fixed
and the average energy fixed.

208
00:11:57,190 --> 00:11:59,620
Two constraints, two multipliers.

209
00:11:59,670 --> 00:12:04,490
Out falls Boltzmann's law: the chance
of a state with energy E is proportional

210
00:12:04,540 --> 00:12:06,660
to e to the minus beta E.

211
00:12:07,210 --> 00:12:09,550
Beta is the multiplier on energy.

212
00:12:09,600 --> 00:12:12,660
It is the price of energy,
measured in entropy.

213
00:12:12,710 --> 00:12:16,640
And physicists have a name for one
over that price: temperature.

214
00:12:18,240 --> 00:12:23,200
The method does come with fine print.
We assumed the gradient of g isn't zero.

215
00:12:23,250 --> 00:12:27,647
The curve y squared equals x cubed
has a sharp cusp at the origin,

216
00:12:27,673 --> 00:12:30,650
and the smallest x on that curve
is right at the cusp.

217
00:12:30,700 --> 00:12:33,410
But there the gradient of g vanishes,

218
00:12:33,460 --> 00:12:36,470
and no multiple of zero can
equal the gradient of f.

219
00:12:36,520 --> 00:12:40,630
The equations have no solution at all,
and they miss the answer completely.

220
00:12:40,680 --> 00:12:43,360
So always check the sharp points by hand.

221
00:12:44,040 --> 00:12:47,070
Also,
tangency only makes a point a candidate.

222
00:12:47,120 --> 00:12:51,500
And if the trail runs off to infinity,
there may be no best point to find.

223
00:12:51,930 --> 00:12:57,050
Real limits are often inequalities too:
spend at most this much.

224
00:12:57,240 --> 00:12:59,850
William Karush worked that out
in his master's thesis

225
00:12:59,900 --> 00:13:03,490
in nineteen thirty-nine,
and Harold Kuhn and Albert Tucker,

226
00:13:03,540 --> 00:13:07,430
unaware of his work, reached the same
result in nineteen fifty-one.

227
00:13:07,560 --> 00:13:09,140
The idea is lovely.

228
00:13:09,190 --> 00:13:12,720
A limit you aren't pressing
against has a price of zero.

229
00:13:14,410 --> 00:13:18,010
Let's go back to the beach,
and let the rope take any shape it likes.

230
00:13:18,060 --> 00:13:23,190
Apply the same idea along the whole rope,
with one multiplier for the entire curve,

231
00:13:23,240 --> 00:13:27,570
and the answer says the rope must
bend by the same amount everywhere.

232
00:13:27,620 --> 00:13:29,060
That is a circle.

233
00:13:29,110 --> 00:13:31,790
Against a straight shore, a semicircle.

234
00:13:32,270 --> 00:13:34,780
Forty meters of rope
now encloses about two

235
00:13:34,820 --> 00:13:39,350
hundred and fifty-five square meters, more
than a quarter better than the rectangle.

236
00:13:39,850 --> 00:13:42,230
And the multiplier?
It is the radius.

237
00:13:42,280 --> 00:13:44,750
Each extra meter of rope buys as many

238
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:48,270
square meters as the radius is long,
about twelve point seven.

239
00:13:48,320 --> 00:13:52,690
Lay a stick from the center to the rope,
and you are holding lambda in your hands.

240
00:13:53,230 --> 00:13:56,420
At the best point,
the only way left to improve is the way

241
00:13:56,460 --> 00:13:58,050
you are forbidden to go.

242
00:13:58,200 --> 00:14:03,060
Lagrange's number tells you exactly
what that forbidden direction is worth.
